    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d250">

                <pb facs="0228" n="178" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav03/pg178"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 661.939/7</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Reinsch</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1918-05-28T15:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 28, 1918, 3
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Warner<anchor n="2" xml:id="d250-f1"/> telegraphs that there is great need of
                    food in the Priamur <pb facs="0229" n="179" xml:id="pg179"/> and says
                    investigation shows it impossible for food to fall into the hands of the enemy
                    when shipped to that region.</p>
                <p>The American Government would be glad to see the embargo lifted from food
                    shipments to Priamur. The Department has received no reply to its telegrams of
                    May 1 to Tokyo, London, and Paris concerning embargo.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d250-f1" n="2" xml:id="d250-f1-content">
                <p>Langdon Warner, Vice Consul at Harbin.</p>
